# Summary
Introduced vulnerable WhatsApp template management endpoints. The underlying generic tenant-access helper appears pre-existing, but this commit adds a new sensitive admin surface that uses it without verifying that the caller's admin role applies to the target tenant.
The new endpoints are registered under tenant_admin_router, which only checks that the caller has an admin-or-higher role in request.state.roles. That role is not tied to the tenant_id path parameter. The subsequent ensure_tenant_access() call allows access to a different tenant whenever has_user_tenant_role() finds any user_tenant_roles row for that tenant, without checking that the row's role is admin/owner. As a result, an authenticated user who has an admin claim for tenant A and any membership in tenant B can call /tenants/{tenantB}/whatsapp/templates endpoints. Once through, the handlers set app.tenant_id to tenant B and can list, create, update, sync, and delete tenant B's WhatsApp templates, including deletion from Meta for live channels. This violates tenant RBAC boundaries and can let lower-privileged users alter outbound messaging readiness and disrupt another tenant's WhatsApp operations.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm tenant_admin_router requires an admin role from request.state.roles but does not bind that role to the tenant_id path parameter.
- [x] Confirm authenticate_request/require_min_role treat roles as session/token-level claims rather than performing a target-tenant role lookup.
- [x] Confirm ensure_tenant_access allows a different path tenant when has_user_tenant_role() finds any membership row, with no role/admin predicate.
- [x] Confirm the new WhatsApp template endpoints call ensure_tenant_access(), then set app.tenant_id to the path tenant and perform sensitive list/update/create/sync/delete operations.
- [x] Dynamically demonstrate that admin-in-tenant-A plus viewer/any-membership-in-tenant-B succeeds for GET and PATCH, while no membership in tenant B is denied.
## Report
Validated the finding with targeted source review plus a FastAPI TestClient PoC against the real registered /v1 routes. This is a Python authorization logic flaw, so crash/Valgrind are not applicable; valgrind and gdb are not installed, and a non-interactive pdb smoke run was attempted. Source evidence: app/api/v1/routes.py:265-268 registers tenant_admin_router with authenticate_request and require_min_role('admin') only. app/core/security.py:188-205 copies token roles into request.state.roles and sets request.state.tenant_id from the token/X-Tenant-Id, without tenant-scoped role lookup. app/core/security.py:233-249 require_min_role() checks only the roles list. app/api/v1/routes.py:354-383 ensure_tenant_access() permits cross-tenant access when has_user_tenant_role() returns true, and has_user_tenant_role() queries only existence of any app.user_tenant_roles row for the target tenant; it does not check role. The new WhatsApp template endpoints then call ensure_tenant_access() and set app.tenant_id to the path tenant before reading/writing, e.g. list at routes.py:1127-1150 and patch at routes.py:1166-1216.

Dynamic reproduction: /workspace/validation_artifacts/copilotoia-whatsapp-rbac-poc/poc_whatsapp_template_cross_tenant_rbac.py creates a FastAPI app including the real routes, overrides only authenticate_request and get_db, and simulates a user with roles=['admin'] scoped to tenant A plus only a low-privilege viewer membership row in tenant B. GET /v1/tenants/{tenantB}/whatsapp/templates returned 200 with tenant B's template. PATCH /v1/tenants/{tenantB}/whatsapp/templates/{template_id} returned 200 and changed status to paused. Logged DB calls show the access check query against app.user_tenant_roles with tenant B but no role predicate, followed by set_config('app.tenant_id', tenantB) and app.whatsapp_templates queries using tenant B. A negative control with no tenant B membership returned 403, confirming the bypass specifically comes from accepting any target-tenant membership while the admin role comes from the other tenant/session.

